   "description": "Lock file opened with O_CREAT|O_TRUNC without O_NOFOLLOW on Unix, so a symlink at the lock path can cause truncation/creation of an arbitrary target file (symlink following / TOCTOU).",
   "file_path": "src/filelock/_unix.py",
   "start_line": 44,
   "end_line": null,
   "start_column": null,
   "end_column": null,
   "code_snippet": "open_flags = os.O_RDWR | os.O_TRUNC\nif not Path(self.lock_file).exists():\n    open_flags |= os.O_CREAT\nfd = os.open(self.lock_file, open_flags, self._context.mode)\nwith suppress(PermissionError):\n    os.fchmod(fd, self._context.mode)",
   "reasoning": "The lock path is checked with Path.exists() and then opened with os.open without O_NOFOLLOW. Between the check and the open, or if the path is already a symlink, os.open follows it. O_TRUNC then truncates whatever file the symlink points to, and os.fchmod changes permissions on it. Exploitation requires write access to the lock file's directory (e.g., a shared /tmp location) to plant the symlink, so the attack is conditional but real.",
   "evidence": {
    "code_context": "UnixFileLock._acquire: ensure_directory_exists(lock_file); open_flags = O_RDWR|O_TRUNC; if not Path(lock_file).exists(): open_flags |= O_CREAT; fd = os.open(lock_file, open_flags, mode); os.fchmod(fd, mode)",
    "dataflow_analysis": "Not traced with tool; the lock_file path is user-configured and passed directly to os.open with no symlink check.",
    "notes": "Matches CodeQL py/lock-open, py/lock-toctou and py/open-no-nofollow results at line 44 (and 46 for fchmod). Only O_NOFOLLOW-style protection is missing."
   },
   "dataflow_path": null,
   "group": "medium_undetermined_medium"
  }
